          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional and relational state. The qualification by her bond limits the claimed extent of that love but does not weaken the utterance's clear avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond and the duties it imposes on her, rather than making a new promise. She identifies herself as owing Lear love, obedience, and honour as his daughter. Lear does not accept this as an adequate performance in his love contest and tells her to amend it, but his rejection does not erase its invocation of filial obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled answer to Lear's demand, carefully qualified by Cordelia's understanding of her bond. Nothing in the represented delivery makes it a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an avowal and invocation of duty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"In this public contest, the expression also functions as a restrained correction of Lear's demand for unlimited declarations: Cordelia refuses competitive exaggeration and precisely limits what she claims. This resistant, corrective function is not fully captured by avowal or obligation alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say who loves him most, explicitly linking their answers to his distribution of royal bounty. After first answering “Nothing” and being ordered to speak again, Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, defining her love by her filial bond rather than competing in unlimited praise.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia does not undertake a new future act. She invokes her already-existing filial bond to Lear and presents love, obedience, and honour as duties she returns to him. Lear rejects the answer as inadequate for the contest and urges her to amend it. The later supplied passages support genuine continuing affection through her concern, prayer, kiss, forgiveness, and distress for her father, but they do not convert the original words into a new promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, specifically Cordelia’s response during Lear’s opening love test.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports the utterance’s avowal and its invocation of filial duty. Cordelia’s private asides and later conduct support actual affection, but the supplied text cannot establish every detail of her inner state. The precise legal or moral terms of the stated “bond” are not exhaustively defined here.\"}"
        }
